# AliasDict (dict subclass)

Values live in the instance; `_alias_map` (alias→key) and `_lookup_map` (key→set(aliases)) are **attrs**, never entries. `_lookup_map` is derived from `_alias_map`.

## Must keep

- Resolve-first via inlined `_alias_map.get(key, key)` in get/set; no `UserDict` / `__missing__` / `_origin` helper.
- Lazy custom `_AliasKeysView` / `_AliasItemsView` — do not rebuild with `dict | dict`, and do not swap in `collections.abc` views (slower iteration).
- `popitem` = FIFO (`next(iter(self))`), not `dict.popitem` LIFO.
- Override `get` / `pop` / `setdefault` / `update` / `__ne__` (C dict skips subclass hooks).
- Pickle via `__reduce_ex__` + `(origins, alias_map)` state; rebuild `_lookup_map` on restore. Copy path copies both maps (do not rebuild lookup from alias_map on copy).

## Origin-only access

```python
# ✅ origins only
dict.__contains__(self, key)
dict.items(self)
dict.__len__(self)

# ❌ includes aliases — wrong for add_alias / merge / copy / pickle
key in self
dict.copy(self)            # non-exact dict path walks keys()/__getitem__
dict.update(dst, other_ad) # same — materializes aliases as real entries
for k, v in other.items():  # when other is AliasDict
```

## Copy / merge

Copy and `|` / `|=` must transfer **origins** with `dict.items(other)`, then copy `_alias_map` and `_lookup_map` (set copies). Iterating an `AliasDict` or calling `update(other_aliasdict)` without care materializes aliases as real keys (or, for `update`, writes origins repeatedly without transferring alias maps — use `|` / `_merge_into` to merge aliases).

## Lookup tradeoff

Resolve-first (`_alias_map.get(key, key)` inlined in hot paths): every get pays an alias_map probe. Faster alias hits; slightly slower origin hits than try-origin-first. Do not flip without re-benchmarking.

## Errors

Raise `KeyError(requested_key)` (not the origin). Keep `AliasError` / `AliasValueError` messages stable.
